course: biology
lesson: 345
title: Rice, maize and potatoes: the plants that feed the world
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Three plants feed a huge share of the world. Rice is a grass that can stand with its feet in water, because air travels down spongy tubes inside its stems to the roots. Flooded paddy fields, built as steps on hills, drown many weeds that cannot cope.
explain: Maize, or corn, is a grass too. Peel back the husk and each long silky thread leads to one kernel; pollen from the feathery tassel on top must land on that silk or the kernel never grows. Count the rows on a cob and you nearly always get an even number.
explain: Potatoes are not seeds or roots but swollen underground stems. They came from the cold Andes mountains of South America, where thousands of purple, red and knobbly kinds still grow.
explain: Every grain of rice or kernel of maize is a seed with a tiny baby plant inside, which is why one grain planted can give back hundreds or even thousands.
fact: Rice can grow in flooded fields because air passes down spongy tubes in its stems to the roots, and flooding drowns many weeds; some farmers keep ducks or fish in the paddy to eat pests and weeds.
fact: Rice is the main food for billions of people, especially in Asia; China and India grow the most, and a field can give two or three crops a year in warm, wet places.
fact: One rice plant makes several stems, each ending in a head of about a hundred grains or more, so one grain planted can give back hundreds or thousands.
fact: White rice is brown rice with the outer layers, called bran, polished off; it cooks faster and keeps longer, but the bran held vitamin B1.
fact: Japanese navy sailors who ate mostly white rice once fell ill with beriberi, an illness that makes the legs weak and numb, until doctors added other foods.
fact: Corn cobs nearly always have an even number of rows, often 12 to 20, because the kernels grow in pairs, and one cob carries about 500 to 800 kernels.
fact: Each silk thread on a maize cob is joined to one kernel, and pollen from the tassel must land on the silk for that kernel to grow; maize is planted in blocks so pollen reaches plenty of silk.
fact: Maize came from teosinte, a wild grass of Mexico whose ear held only about a dozen hard kernels; farmers changed it over thousands of years by keeping seed from their best plants.
fact: Maize now grows on every continent except Antarctica, and much of it feeds farm animals.
fact: Potatoes were first farmed in the Andes of South America perhaps 7000 or more years ago, grow well in cold, high places, and were brought to Europe by the Spanish in the 1500s.
fact: A potato turned green in the light has also made a bitter natural poison called solanine, so green parts and sprouts should be cut away and potatoes stored in the dark.
fact: Potatoes are cousins of tomatoes, peppers and aubergines; the small green fruits on a potato plant look like little tomatoes but are poisonous, and farmers grow potatoes from seed potatoes.
exercise: How can rice grow with its roots under water? | Air travels down spongy tubes in its stems to the roots | Its roots can breathe water through tiny gills, just like a fish does | It holds its breath until the water dries | The water brings it all the air it needs
exercise: Why do rice farmers flood their fields? | Flooding drowns many weeds | Rice only grows in the dark | Flooding keeps the frogs nice and warm | Water turns the grains white
exercise: Why do some rice farmers keep ducks in the paddy? | The ducks eat pests and weeds | The ducks plant the rice | The ducks keep the water warm | The ducks guard the field from thieves
exercise: Which two countries grow the most rice? | China and India | Britain and France | Canada and Russia | Egypt and Argentina
exercise: About how many grains are on one head of a rice stem? | About a hundred or more | About three | About ten thousand | Exactly one
exercise: What is the difference between brown rice and white rice? | White rice has had the bran polished off | Brown rice is just white rice that got burnt in the pan | They are two different plants | White rice grows in snow
exercise: What illness did sailors get from eating mostly white rice? | Beriberi | Seasickness | Whooping cough | Chickenpox
exercise: Why does a corn cob nearly always have an even number of rows? | The kernels grow in pairs from the start | Farmers go round and pull off every odd row by hand | Even rows are easier to eat | Odd rows fall off in the wind
exercise: What is each silk thread on a corn cob joined to? | One kernel | The roots | The tassel | The leaves
exercise: Why is maize planted in blocks instead of one long row? | So pollen can drift onto plenty of silk | So big tractors have room to turn round at the end | So the cobs grow in the shade | So the birds cannot find it
exercise: Which wild grass did maize come from? | Teosinte from Mexico | Wild wheat from Iraq | Bamboo from China | Sugar cane from Brazil
exercise: Where were potatoes first farmed? | In the Andes of South America | In Ireland, where people ate them every day | In the deserts of Africa | In the forests of Russia
exercise: Who brought potatoes to Europe in the 1500s? | The Spanish | The Vikings of Norway | The ancient Romans | Chinese traders
exercise: What has a potato made if it turns green under the skin? | A bitter natural poison called solanine | Lots of extra vitamins from the sunshine | Sugar for sweetness | A new seed
exercise: Which plant is a close cousin of the potato? | The tomato | The banana plant | The apple tree | The oak tree
exercise: Why should you never eat the small green fruits on a potato plant? | They are poisonous | They are far too sour to eat | They give you hiccups | They are where the potatoes grow
test: Count them: a corn cob has 16 rows with 40 kernels in each. How many kernels and how many silk threads did it once have? | 640 kernels and 640 silks | 640 kernels and 16 silks | 56 kernels and 56 silks | 640 kernels and 40 silks
test: «found a cob with 15 rows, bet that's normal» What is the best answer? | It would be very unusual, because kernels grow in pairs | Yes, odd numbers of rows are the most common kind of all | All cobs have exactly 15 rows | Rows have nothing to do with pairs
test: A farmer finds a cob with lots of gaps where kernels are missing. What does it tell him? | Those silks got no pollen | The cob was too cold | Mice stole the kernels at night | The cob grew too fast
test: If one grain of rice gives a thousand, how many would ten grains give? | Ten thousand | One thousand | One hundred | Ten
test: «spuds are just roots innit» What is the true answer? | No, a potato is a swollen underground stem | Yes, they are roots, the same as carrots and parsnips | No, they are seeds | No, they are fruits
test: In a cartoon, a cheeky potato with eyes and arms sprouts in a sunny window and the kid eats it raw, green bits and all. What is the danger? | Green parts and sprouts hold a natural poison that can upset your tummy | Nothing, green potatoes are healthiest | The eyes will see inside your tummy | Raw potatoes turn into chips inside you
test: Why should a sack of potatoes be kept in a dark cupboard? | Light makes them turn green and produce poison | The dark makes them grow much bigger in the sack | Potatoes are afraid of light | Light melts their skins
test: «white rice is better, it's cleaner» What is the fairest reply? | White rice cooks faster and keeps longer, but the bran it lost held vitamin B1 | White rice has more vitamins than brown, because the polishing adds extra goodness to it | Brown rice is just dirty white rice | They are exactly the same in every way
test: How did farmers without science books turn a scruffy wild grass into big soft corn cobs? | They kept seeds from their best plants year after year for thousands of years | They poured special magic water on the wild grass every morning until it grew cobs | They crossed it with apple trees | A single farmer did it in one summer
test: Why might a plant that keeps its food underground cope well with cold mountain weather? | The soil protects its stored food from frost | Mountain air is always warm, even at the very top | Underground plants do not need water | Cold makes potatoes grow faster
test: Why are rice fields on hills built as steps? | Each flat step can hold water | Steps keep the rice dry | Steps make it easier to climb | Rice grows only on stairs
test: In Ice Age, Scrat chases an acorn. If he had a corn cob instead and planted every kernel, what would grow? | A maize plant from each kernel, because each kernel is a seed | Nothing would grow, because corn kernels are only food and not seeds | One big oak tree | A field of potatoes
exam: Identify the means by which the rice plant obtains air for its roots while standing in flooded fields. | Air is conveyed downward through spongy tissue within the stems | The roots extract oxygen from the water as a fish does | The plant does not require air at any stage | Air is supplied by the farmer through pipes
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the rows of kernels upon a cob of maize are ordinarily even in number. | The kernels develop in pairs from the outset | Odd rows are removed by the farmer | Odd rows are shed in the wind | The number is determined by the colour of the cob
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of the potato? | It was first cultivated in the Andes of South America and brought to Europe by the Spanish in the sixteenth century | It was first cultivated in Ireland | It was first cultivated in the deserts of North Africa | It was brought to Europe by the Romans
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning white rice. | It is brown rice from which the bran has been removed, with a consequent loss of vitamin B1 | It is a distinct species cultivated in cold regions | It contains more vitamins than brown rice | It is brown rice that has been bleached with chalk
